{
  "concept": "A slow neutron absorbed by uranium-235 forms the unstable U-236 nucleus, which deforms and splits into Ba-141 and Kr-92 plus three neutrons and a large burst of energy — and those three neutrons can trigger further fissions, sustaining a chain reaction.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Incoming neutron: single small grey sphere at far left, labeled with subscript/superscript notation 1 over 0, n",
    "Black arrow from incoming neutron toward the U-235 nucleus, with short pale impact streaks at the point of collision",
    "U-235 nucleus: large roughly spherical cluster of interlocking green (protons) and pale grey/white (neutrons) shaded spheres, label 235 over 92, U beneath it",
    "Black arrow from U-235 to the intermediate nucleus",
    "U-236 intermediate: elongated, pinched dumbbell-shaped cluster of the same green/grey spheres, outlined in a thin dark red contour to signal instability, labels '236 over 92, U' and 'Unstable nucleus' beneath",
    "Two black arrows fanning up-right and down-right from the pinched nucleus to the two daughter nuclei",
    "Kr-92 daughter nucleus: smaller green/grey sphere cluster upper right, label 92 over 36, Kr",
    "Ba-141 daughter nucleus: slightly larger green/grey sphere cluster lower right, label 141 over 56, Ba",
    "Gold/yellow multi-point starburst between the two daughter nuclei with the word 'Energy' at its center",
    "Three small grey neutron spheres at far right, each reached by its own short black arrow, grouped with the label '3, 1 over 0, n'",
    "Balanced nuclear equation across the bottom: 235/92 U + 1/0 n → 236/92 U → 141/56 Ba + 92/36 Kr + 3 1/0 n, with arrows between terms",
    "Legend/key for sphere coloring: green = proton, grey = neutron",
    "Info panel (appears on click) showing symbol, atomic number Z, mass number A, and chain-reaction role of the selected particle",
    "Chain-reaction overlay: a branching tree of fission events (1 → 3 → 9 → 27) drawn as small nucleus icons connected by neutron arrows, with a generation counter and cumulative-energy readout"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "fissionPhase",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Fission stage",
      "states": ["neutron approach", "capture: U-236 forms", "deformation & scission", "products + energy released"],
      "default": "neutron approach",
      "teaches": "Fission is not one event but a sequence: absorption first creates a heavier, deformed compound nucleus, and only then does it break apart."
    },
    {
      "id": "playFission",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Play full fission sequence",
      "loop": false,
      "teaches": "The real timescale: neutron capture, nuclear elongation, and scission happen as one continuous, extremely fast motion ending in a burst of energy and flying neutrons."
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectParticle",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each species has a definite Z and A, and summing them across the equation shows mass number and charge are conserved; the three free neutrons are what make the process self-propagating."
    },
    {
      "id": "chainGeneration",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Chain-reaction generations",
      "range": [0, 3, 1],
      "default": 0,
      "teaches": "Because each fission releases three neutrons, the number of fission events multiplies geometrically (1, 3, 9, 27), which is why one neutron can unleash enormous energy."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A single slow neutron",
      "narration": "One low-energy neutron drifts toward a uranium-235 nucleus. Click the neutron to see that it carries mass number 1 and zero charge — it can approach without electrostatic repulsion.",
      "state": { "fissionPhase": "neutron approach", "chainGeneration": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Capture forms U-236",
      "narration": "The nucleus absorbs the neutron, becoming uranium-236. The red outline marks a compound nucleus with too much internal energy to survive.",
      "state": { "fissionPhase": "capture: U-236 forms", "chainGeneration": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Deformation and scission",
      "narration": "The excited nucleus stretches into a pinched dumbbell as repulsion between the two halves overcomes the strong force holding it together.",
      "state": { "fissionPhase": "deformation & scission", "chainGeneration": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Products, neutrons, and energy",
      "narration": "The nucleus splits into Ba-141 and Kr-92, ejecting three neutrons and releasing a huge burst of energy. Check the equation: 92 = 56 + 36 and 236 = 141 + 92 + 3.",
      "state": { "fissionPhase": "products + energy released", "chainGeneration": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "One fission becomes three",
      "narration": "Each of the three emitted neutrons can strike another U-235 nucleus. Generation 1 turns one fission into three.",
      "state": { "fissionPhase": "products + energy released", "chainGeneration": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The chain runs away",
      "narration": "Advance the generations: 1 → 3 → 9 → 27 fissions, with energy released multiplying just as fast. This geometric growth is the self-sustaining chain reaction behind reactors and weapons.",
      "state": { "fissionPhase": "products + energy released", "chainGeneration": 3 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Fission both consumes one neutron and emits three — that surplus is what turns a single nuclear event into an exponentially growing chain reaction.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Left-to-right flow on a white background in three columns: reactant (U-235 struck by a neutron), intermediate (pinched U-236 outlined in dark red, labeled 'Unstable nucleus'), and products (Kr-92 upper right, Ba-141 lower right, three neutrons at far right, gold starburst labeled 'Energy' between the daughters). All nuclei are drawn as space-filling clusters of shaded spheres: medium green for protons, pale grey-white for neutrons, with soft highlights giving a 3D ball look; U-235 is the largest cluster, Ba-141 slightly larger than Kr-92. Black arrows connect stages, with small pale impact streaks at the neutron strike point. Isotope labels use standard superscript-mass/subscript-atomic-number notation to the left of each element symbol. The balanced equation sits centered along the bottom in the same notation. When a particle is clicked, dim the rest of the figure and show a compact info card near it. The chain-reaction overlay replaces the right column with a branching tree of small U-235 icons, three neutron arrows per node, dimming earlier generations and showing a generation and cumulative-fission counter."
}

