      "state": {
        "fissionPhase": "products + energy released",
        "chainGeneration": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "The chain runs away",
      "narration": "Advance the generations: 1 → 3 → 9 → 27 fissions, with energy released multiplying just as fast. This geometric growth is the self-sustaining chain reaction behind reactors and weapons.",
      "state": {
        "fissionPhase": "products + energy released",
        "chainGeneration": 3
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Fission both consumes one neutron and emits three — that surplus is what turns a single nuclear event into an exponentially growing chain reaction.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Left-to-right flow on a white background in three columns: reactant (U-235 struck by a neutron), intermediate (pinched U-236 outlined in dark red, labeled 'Unstable nucleus'), and products (Kr-92 upper right, Ba-141 lower right, three neutrons at far right, gold starburst labeled 'Energy' between the daughters). All nuclei are drawn as space-filling clusters of shaded spheres: medium green for protons, pale grey-white for neutrons, with soft highlights giving a 3D ball look; U-235 is the largest cluster, Ba-141 slightly larger than Kr-92. Black arrows connect stages, with small pale impact streaks at the neutron strike point. Isotope labels use standard superscript-mass/subscript-atomic-number notation to the left of each element symbol. The balanced equation sits centered along the bottom in the same notation. When a particle is clicked, dim the rest of the figure and show a compact info card near it. The chain-reaction overlay replaces the right column with a branching tree of small U-235 icons, three neutron arrows per node, dimming earlier generations and showing a generation and cumulative-fission counter."
}

